## 2. Team Tracker

- Project: slug `{{SLUG}}`, `project_id` **{{PROJECT_ID}}** in `tt_projects` (Supabase
  `ntjzghsbrzkvpkniotaj`). Open a working session with `/proiect {{SLUG}}`.
- **UI Coverage is the site map.** Every page and section of this product is a
  `tt_ui_surfaces` row with a `purpose`. `docs/sitemap.md` is a dated snapshot of the
  intake; when they disagree, the tracker is right. A section that exists in the tracker
  but not in code is `planned`; the first construction session on its page builds the page
  skeleton (route, layout, navigation entry, labelled empty places for the other `planned`
  sections) and then the section itself.
- **Human gates are never written by agents.** `manual_verdict`, `verdict_fingerprint`,
  `spec_approved_at`, `shipped_at` on `tt_ui_surfaces` and `launch_stage` on the delivery
  profile are set only by the human through the Team Tracker UI. A trigger rejects agent
  writes to them; do not look for a way around it — leave the section one click away and
  say which click.
- **Verification in the browser is a gate, not a courtesy.** Nothing UI is done until it was
  opened in the preview (`.claude/launch.json` → `{{PREVIEW_NAME}}`, port {{PREVIEW_PORT}})
  at the platform viewports — site: 1440 / 768 / 375; app: 375 — with screenshots as
  evidence. "It compiles" is not a verdict.
- **Bugbot before merge.** Every PR gets a clean GitHub Bugbot review before it lands on
  `main`; a finding is fixed or explicitly answered, never ignored.
- **Branch names**: `fix/bug-<id>-<slug>`, `feat/feature-<id>-<slug>`,
  `ui/section-<id>-<slug>` — the kind and id are the tracker's, so the branch says which row
  it closes. One branch, one tracker item.
- `npm test` runs `check:rules` first, so Bugbot and CI see the rule suite too.

