## 4. Folder Structure

{{FOLDER_TREE}}

**Where what lives** — a new file goes into the folder above that names its kind; a file
without a folder is a signal that the kind is new, and that is a decision, not a drop into
the root. Pages in `pages/`, reusable UI in `components/` (the catalog in `components/ui/`
is the only place a button, input, card, dialog or toast is styled), pure logic and data
access in `lib/`, hooks in `hooks/`, styles only in `styles/`. Group by feature when a
folder grows: `components/contact/` rather than `components/ContactMapTile.tsx` next to
thirty others. **Max ~20 files per folder** — `check:rules` fails above that.

## 5. Design System

Full reference: `docs/design-system.md`. The short version every change must respect:

- **`src/styles/tokens.css` is the single source.** Colours, spacing, type scale, radii,
  shadows and fonts are CSS variables there; `global.css` maps them into Tailwind
  (Vite + React) or exposes them to plain CSS (Astro). **No literal colour or size in a
  component** — no `#hex`, no `rgb()`, no `[13px]`, no `bg-blue-500`; `check:rules` fails on
  the first one. A value that is not in `tokens.css` is a design decision first.
- **Canonical catalog** (`components/ui/`): `Button` (primary · secondary · ghost · danger;
  sm · md; `loading`), `Input`, `Select`, `Card`, `Section`, `Dialog`, `Toast`
  (`useToast`), `EmptyState`, `Spinner`. **Never** invent a second button, input or dialog;
  extend the catalog component with a variant if one is genuinely missing, and record why.
- **Colour roles**: `bg` page · `surface` cards/inputs/dialogs · `text` / `text-muted` ·
  `primary` (+ `primary-contrast`) for the one action colour · `accent` for highlights only ·
  `danger` for destructive and errors · `border`. Tailwind classes: `bg-bg`, `bg-surface`,
  `text-text`, `text-muted`, `bg-primary text-primary-contrast`, `border-border`.
- **Type scale**: `text-xs` (12) badges · `text-sm` (14) metadata, labels · `text-base` (16)
  body, inputs, buttons · `text-lg` (18) card titles · `text-xl` (20) dialog titles ·
  `text-2xl` (24) section titles · `text-3xl` (30) page title. Nothing outside these seven.
- **Icons**: inline SVG (Heroicons outline style, `stroke="currentColor"`, `strokeWidth={2}`),
  sized with `size-4` / `size-5`. Never emoji.
- **Popups** (`Dialog`, dropdowns, toasts) are `z-50` and never rendered inside an
  `overflow-hidden` / `overflow-auto` parent.
- **Common mistakes** — each one is a `check:rules` failure or a `/ui-audit` finding:
  1. A literal colour or pixel size in a component instead of a token.
  2. A new button style instead of a `Button` variant.
  3. A font size outside the scale (`text-[13px]`, `text-4xl`).
  4. Emoji as icons.
  5. A popup inside an `overflow` container, or without `z-50`.
  6. `accent` used for an action, or two `primary` buttons in one view.
  7. A list with no `EmptyState`.

## 6. Code Rules (STRICT — all verified by `npm run check:rules`)

### Project organization comes first
Organization is a top priority — treat it as part of every change, not an afterthought.

- **Put files where they belong** (section 4). Do not dump files in the project root or in a
  random folder because it was convenient.
- **Group by feature when a folder grows.** Max ~20 files per folder; past that, split into
  feature subfolders. A flat folder with 40 files is a code smell the rule suite reports.
- **One responsibility per file.** A component file holds one main component; extract
  sub-components, helpers and types into their own files rather than stacking them.
- **Keep names consistent**: components and pages `PascalCase.tsx`, hooks `useX.ts`, lib
  `camelCase.ts`, routes kebab-case in Romanian (`/despre-noi`), DB types prefixed `Db`.
  A misnamed or misplaced file is an organization bug.

### No duplicate code
Before writing a helper or a component, **search `components/` and `lib/`** for one that
does the job; **extend it, do not fork it.** `jscpd` runs inside `check:rules` with a low
threshold — two copies of the same ten lines fail the suite.

### Delete what is no longer used
Dead code rots the project. Actively remove things we no longer use — do not leave them
"just in case".

- **Delete unused files**: old components, pages, hooks, helpers, experiments, and
  `.bak` / `-copy` / `-old` / `-v2` duplicates, once superseded.
- **Delete unused code**: imports, variables, props, functions, exports, types and CSS
  classes — removed, not commented out.
- **Remove dead routes and assets**, and stale config nothing reads.
- **When you replace something, remove the old version in the same change.**
- **Before deleting, confirm it is truly unused** — search for imports, route registrations
  and string usages.

### No comments in code
Do NOT write comments in `.ts` / `.tsx` / `.astro` / `.css`. No inline comments, no block
comments, no JSDoc, no TODO comments, no commented-out code. The code must be
self-explanatory through naming and structure; the *why* goes where section 7 says.
`check:rules` uses the TypeScript scanner, so there are no exceptions and no escape hatch.

### Maximum 600 lines per file
600 is the hard ceiling, not the target — aim far lower. Past it, split into focused
modules.

### When reviewing code, always verify
1. Zero comments in changed or new files.
2. No file over 600 lines; no folder over ~20 files.
3. Every new file is in the right folder with the right name.
4. No dead code, unused imports/exports, commented-out blocks or orphaned files.
5. When something was replaced, the old version is gone.
6. No literal colour or size outside `tokens.css`; only catalog components for UI primitives.
7. Every UI Coverage section is rendered through `Section` with its stable key (section 8).
8. `AGENTS.md` equals `CLAUDE.md`.

## 7. Where the "why" lives

Comments are banned, so the reasoning cannot sit next to the code. It lives in exactly three
places, and a change without a *why* in one of them is incomplete:

1. **Commit messages** — conventional (`feat:` / `fix:` / `ui:` / `docs:` / `chore:`), short
   imperative subject, and a body that says what was tried and why this shape won. The
   body is the primary record for decisions local to one change.
2. **`docs/superpowers/specs/`** — one dated design spec per feature or non-trivial change,
   written before the code; implementation plans in `docs/superpowers/plans/`.
3. **Section 10, Decisions** — one line per decision that outlives a single change (a
   token added, a component variant, a dependency, a rule exception refused).

## 8. Section ↔ code

Every section in UI Coverage is one component rendered through `Section` with
`data-section="<stable key>"`, so `/ui-audit` finds it in the DOM without guessing and
recognises it as the same surface the tracker already knows:

- Stable key shape: `{{CODEBASE}}:section:<normalized label>:{{CODEBASE}}:page:<route>` —
  e.g. `{{CODEBASE}}:section:hero:{{CODEBASE}}:page:/`, `{{CODEBASE}}:section:harta:{{CODEBASE}}:page:/contact`.
  `normalize` lowercases, strips diacritics and turns runs of other characters into `-`.
- The key is copied from the tracker row (or from `docs/sitemap.md`), never typed from
  memory; a key that does not match creates a duplicate surface at the next audit.
- Shared chrome (header, footer, navigation) is one canonical unit on the layout hub page,
  not repeated per page.
- A `planned` section that is not built yet stays a labelled empty place in the page
  skeleton so the page's structure is visible before every section exists.

